From c8012f51e194e9f9198bd53a90792f3e7bee1dba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 18:38:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add a step-by-step SMI simulation walkthrough

The Monte Carlo package could be run but not audited: gen_montecarlo.m does
the whole SMI arm in one call, and an outsider had no way to see what any
step produced or to check it independently.

notebooks/smi_simulation_walkthrough.m walks the same arm one step at a
time -- protocol, ground truth, kernel, forward convolution, Rician noise,
SMI.fit, peaks -- and ends every step with a CHECK that compares its output
against something computed a DIFFERENT way, printing ok or ** FAILED **
next to the number. Reading only the CHECK lines is a complete audit. The
ones that are not self-confirming:

  zonal response == SMI forward model    4.5e-16
  harmonics vs direct convolution        1.6e-03  (band limiting, explained)
  spherical mean is orientation-blind    7.4e-07
  recovered sigma                        0.6% off the value that was typed in
  the ceiling                            the same peak finder on the noise-free truth
  sh2peaks cross-check                   45 deg: 82.0% vs the report's 81.1%

It reads its constants from mc_config.m, so it cannot drift from the
pipeline, and it is a plain .m with publish-style markup: it runs in MATLAB
and Octave and converts to a Live Script with no edits. .mlx is deliberately
not the stored form -- zipped XML cannot be diffed, reviewed in a pull
request, or run under Octave, which is the opposite of the point.

Two outputs are findings rather than failures and are called out in place,
because both look alarming otherwise: the band-limited ground truth is
negative over ~40% of the sphere (truncation ringing, so the non-negativity
constraint is a regularizer and not a statement of fact), and the 15 degree
crossing is resolved 0% of the time because the noise-free truth also comes
back as a single lobe.

Also adds protocol/hcp_like_3shell.txt, the gradient table as tracked text,
so the MATLAB side needs no Python. setup_protocol.py now writes it at full
double precision; regenerating it reproduces the tracked copy byte for byte,
verified against the table the published run used.

